<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="no"?>
<!--
    Breed husbandry traits. Every trait is a multiplier, 1 = unchanged.

    feed     Daily food, on top of the subtype's food curve, metabolism, pregnancy and lactation.
    growth   Weight gained per hour of growth. Losses are not scaled.
    femaleGrowth    Growth of a female relative to a male, default 0.6.
    matureAgeMonth  Time scale of growth, in months. Base gain is (target weight - birth weight) /
                    matureAgeMonth per month; growth, femaleGrowth, age and feed then change the
                    actual rate, so only a male with growth 1 on full feed is on target by then.
                    The expected weight for age that food, sale value and newly generated
                    animals are measured against reaches the target at this age.
                    Default 1.5 x the subtype's reproduction age.
    grass    How well the breed uses what its pen ate from each forage category below. The pen's
    hay      food factor is weighted by the share of each category in the last feeding, so a
    silage   Highland on grass grows as if the grass were 25% richer. Health, milk and the other
             outputs still use the pen's plain food factor.

    A trait is looked up by subType name first, then by breed within the animal type, then by
    animal type, then on the root element.

    Root settings:
    growthNutritionFactor  Growth runs at min(food factor x forage efficiency x this, 1). At 1.10,
                           grass (0.40) grows at 44%, hay (0.65) 71.5%, silage (0.80) 88% and
                           TMR (1.00) at full speed.
    bodyWeightExponent     Daily food is scaled by (weight / expected weight for its age) ^ this,
    minBodyRatio           the ratio clamped to minBodyRatio..maxBodyRatio. The expected weight
    maxBodyRatio           grows from the subtype's minWeight to the animal's own target weight.

    A pen with its own meadow that ate nothing from the trough in a feeding counts as on grass.

    Animals the dealer generates are never heavier than their own target weight. What ages and
    breeding states the dealer sells is in xml/dealerStock.xml.
    Birth weight (RLBirthWeight). Mean weight of a single young from an average dam:
    birthWeightRatio  x the target weight of the breed's female (0.062 = 6.2%). Each configured
                      subtype's minWeight is set to this mean at load, so growth starts there.
    birthFemaleKg     The mean in kg, in place of the ratio (pigs). It is the same number as the
                      breed's weightProfiles.xml profile.
    maleBirthFactor   x for a male young.
    One birth's mean is then scaled by:
    litter2/3/4       per young in a litter of 2, 3, 4 and more;
    litterBase, litterStep, litterMin, litterMax
                      instead, for litter animals: 1 - litterStep x (litter - litterBase),
                      clamped to litterMin..litterMax;
    firstBirthFactor  for the dam's first birth;
                      the dam's condition, (weight / expected weight for her age) ^ 0.5 clamped
                      to 0.85..1.05; for a cross, the mean of both breeds; and the young's
                      metabolism gene, 1 + 0.05 x (metabolism - 1).
    birthWeightCV     The weight is drawn from a normal distribution with this coefficient of
                      variation around the mean, truncated at 2.5 standard deviations.
    birthSurvivalBase Baseline chance to survive birth for an average-weight, average-health young.
    birthHealthEffect Health gene modifier: survival x (1 + effect x (health - 1)).
    birthLowWeightSD  Low-weight risk starts this many SD below the birth mean.
    birthLowWeightPenalty  Survival lost per further SD below that threshold.
    birthHighWeightSD High-weight/dystocia risk starts this many SD above the mean.
    birthHighWeightPenalty Survival lost per further SD above that threshold; set 0 to disable.
    A subtype with no birthWeightRatio (the map's ducks, geese, pets) keeps its minWeight.

    Wool shearing season (woolShearing, RLWoolShearing). A fleece grows all year (each sheep's
    wool curve x woolScale above, productivity, health); this only sets when it becomes pallets.
    fillTypes         Pallet fill types that are sheep wool.
    preset            DEFAULT, and map presets (maps: map mod names) that apply on those maps.
      mode            YEAR_ROUND: the wool goes to the pen's pallets every hour (the old way).
                      SPRING_SHEARING: each sheep keeps the wool it grows (saved with it) and the
                      pen is shorn once, in the first hour of a shearingPeriods month in which it
                      holds wool, at least intervalMonths after its last shearing; the shearing
                      gives exactly the wool grown since, so a year's total is YEAR_ROUND's.
      shearingPeriods EARLY_SPRING .. LATE_WINTER (period 1 = early spring).
    Hof Bergmann shears in late spring / early summer (May-June, as in Germany and the UK); a
    stall set to manual shearing at its shearing station is left to the map. A copy of this file
    without woolShearing uses these same presets.

    To change these without editing the mod, copy this file to
    modSettings/FS25_RealisticLivestockRM/breedTraits.xml; that copy then replaces this file
    entirely. In multiplayer the server's file applies.
-->
<breedTraits growthNutritionFactor="1.10" bodyWeightExponent="0.35" minBodyRatio="0.5" maxBodyRatio="1.5"
             birthWeightCV="0.10" birthSurvivalBase="0.97" birthHealthEffect="0.20"
             birthLowWeightSD="1.5" birthLowWeightPenalty="0.10"
             birthHighWeightSD="2.0" birthHighWeightPenalty="0">
    <forage>
        <category name="grass" fillTypes="GRASS_WINDROW"/>
        <category name="hay" fillTypes="DRYGRASS_WINDROW CLOVER_WINDROW"/>
        <category name="silage" fillTypes="SILAGE FORAGE FORAGEPLUS ROUNDBALE"/>
    </forage>

    <woolShearing fillTypes="WOOL SHEEPWOOL_SHEARED">
        <preset name="DEFAULT" mode="YEAR_ROUND"/>
        <preset name="HOF_BERGMANN" maps="FS25_HofBergmann" mode="SPRING_SHEARING" shearingPeriods="LATE_SPRING EARLY_SUMMER" intervalMonths="12"/>
    </woolShearing>

    <!-- Calves: 5-6.5% of the cow, bull calves ~7% heavier, twins 85% each, heifers' first 93%. -->
    <animalType name="COW" maleBirthFactor="1.07" birthWeightCV="0.10" firstBirthFactor="0.93"
                litter2="0.85" litter3="0.75" litter4="0.70"
                birthSurvivalBase="0.97" birthHealthEffect="0.20"
                birthLowWeightSD="1.8" birthLowWeightPenalty="0.10"
                birthHighWeightSD="1.3" birthHighWeightPenalty="0.12">
        <breed name="HIGHLAND"      feed="0.80" growth="0.90" grass="1.25" hay="1.15" silage="1.00" birthWeightRatio="0.054" milkCollect="0" sucklingMonths="7"/>   <!-- 27 / 29 kg -->
        <breed name="HEREFORD"      feed="0.90" growth="1.02" grass="1.15" hay="1.10" silage="1.00" birthWeightRatio="0.0567" milkCollect="0" sucklingMonths="7"/>  <!-- 34 / 36 kg -->
        <breed name="WATER_BUFFALO" feed="0.90" growth="0.96" grass="1.15" hay="1.05" silage="1.00" birthWeightRatio="0.0618"/>  <!-- 34 / 36 kg -->
        <breed name="LIMOUSIN"      feed="0.95" growth="1.08" grass="0.95" hay="1.00" silage="1.05" birthWeightRatio="0.0585" milkCollect="0" sucklingMonths="7"/>  <!-- 38 / 41 kg -->
        <breed name="ANGUS"         feed="1.00" growth="1.00" grass="1.00" hay="1.00" silage="1.00" birthWeightRatio="0.0492" milkCollect="0" sucklingMonths="7"/>  <!-- 32 / 34 kg, easy calving -->
        <breed name="SWISS_BROWN"   feed="1.00" growth="1.00" grass="1.05" hay="1.05" silage="1.00" birthWeightRatio="0.0615"/>  <!-- 40 / 43 kg -->
        <breed name="HOLSTEIN"      feed="1.10" growth="0.95" grass="0.90" hay="0.95" silage="1.10" birthWeightRatio="0.0618"/>  <!-- 42 / 45 kg -->

        <!--
            sucklingMonths: a suckler (beef) breed's suckling period in months after calving. Her calves are weaned from
            her at this age and she is dried off then: her lactation ends (RLLactation; other cows milk RLLactation.MONTHS,
            10). The dealer never offers the breed younger than this age, whatever the purpose, so a bought beef calf
            needs no milk. Unset: not a suckler breed (a dairy breed's calves are taken off early, to calf rearing
            pens). What a calf at its dam may drink at each age is still its own allowance (calfRearing.xml). Spring-born
            suckler calves are weaned from about 6 months and sold as weanlings at 5-12 months (AHDB, "Successful calf
            weaning"); 7 is one default for every suckler breed. Set on the breed, so its bull and heifer calves have it
            too; at most 10. The same eleven breeds as milkCollect="0".
        -->
        <breed name="CHAROLAIS_PACK"      sucklingMonths="7"/>
        <breed name="REDANGUS_PACK"       sucklingMonths="7"/>
        <breed name="SHORTHORN_PACK"      sucklingMonths="7"/>
        <breed name="IRISHMOILED_PACK"    sucklingMonths="7"/>
        <breed name="BRITISHBLUE_PACK"    sucklingMonths="7"/>
        <breed name="BELTEDGALLOWAY_PACK" sucklingMonths="7"/>
        <breed name="SIMMENTAL_PACK"      sucklingMonths="7"/>
    </animalType>

    <!-- Gilts grow almost as fast as boars; the mod's 0.6 for females is for breeding stock. -->
    <!-- Piglets (pig P4, 2026-10-16, real sources in FS25_PigBreeds_离线准备_2026-10-16/生长数据核对_v2.md): the mean in kg,
         not a share of the sow (a piglet's weight does not follow her size). Landrace 1.52 kg both sexes (Canadian purebred),
         Berkshire 1.42 / 1.47 (Korean herd LS means, also the boar factor 1.035), Black pied as the game loaded it (no breed
         data). One piglet more is 35 g lighter (Quiniou et al. 2002): 2.4% of the mean at a litter of 12. Spread CV 0.20
         (population CV 0.20-0.21). Old ratios gave 1.632 / 1.418 / 1.444 kg; animals already born keep their weight. -->
    <animalType name="PIG" femaleGrowth="0.95" maleBirthFactor="1.035" birthWeightCV="0.20"
                litterBase="12" litterStep="0.024" litterMin="0.80" litterMax="1.15"
                birthSurvivalBase="0.94" birthHealthEffect="0.20"
                birthLowWeightSD="0.9" birthLowWeightPenalty="0.20"
                birthHighWeightSD="2.5" birthHighWeightPenalty="0">
        <breed name="BERKSHIRE"  feed="1.08" growth="0.95" matureAgeMonth="18.9" birthFemaleKg="1.42"/>   <!-- 118 kg at 6.5 months; born 1.42 / 1.47 kg -->
        <breed name="LANDRACE"   feed="1.00" growth="1.05" matureAgeMonth="20.1" birthFemaleKg="1.494"/>  <!-- 126 kg at 5.5 months; born 1.494 / 1.546 kg -->
        <breed name="BLACK_PIED" feed="0.90" growth="0.95" matureAgeMonth="15.1" birthFemaleKg="1.444"/>  <!-- 120 kg at 6 months; born 1.444 / 1.494 kg -->
    </animalType>

    <!-- Lambs and kids (goats share the SHEEP type): single 6-8% of the ewe, twins 85%, triplets 72%.
         femaleGrowth 0.76: ewe lambs grow at 224 against ram lambs' 293 g a day (sex trials, sheep S3, 2026-10-11); goats keep 0.6. -->
    <animalType name="SHEEP" femaleGrowth="0.76" maleBirthFactor="1.07" birthWeightCV="0.12" firstBirthFactor="0.93"
                litter2="0.85" litter3="0.72" litter4="0.65"
                birthSurvivalBase="0.97" birthHealthEffect="0.20"
                birthLowWeightSD="1.2" birthLowWeightPenalty="0.15"
                birthHighWeightSD="1.8" birthHighWeightPenalty="0.05">
        <breed name="BLACK_WELSH"    feed="0.85" growth="0.90" birthWeightRatio="0.080"/>   <!-- 4.0 / 4.3 kg -->
        <breed name="LANDRACE"       feed="0.95" growth="1.00" birthWeightRatio="0.07273"/> <!-- 6.4 / 6.85 kg, unchanged when the ewe went 80 -> 88 kg -->
        <breed name="STEINSCHAF"     feed="1.05" growth="1.05" birthWeightRatio="0.060"/>   <!-- 3.3 / 3.5 kg -->
        <breed name="SWISS_MOUNTAIN" feed="1.10" growth="1.05" birthWeightRatio="0.08347"/> <!-- 6.0 / 6.4 kg, unchanged when the ewe went 85 -> 72 kg -->
        <breed name="GOAT"           feed="0.85" growth="0.95" grass="1.15" hay="1.15" birthWeightRatio="0.0583" femaleGrowth="0.6"/>  <!-- 3.8 / 4.1 kg -->
    </animalType>

    <!-- Foals: 10% of the mare, twins 80% each. -->
    <animalType name="HORSE" birthWeightRatio="0.10" maleBirthFactor="1.04" birthWeightCV="0.10"
                firstBirthFactor="0.93" litter2="0.80"
                birthSurvivalBase="0.97" birthHealthEffect="0.20"
                birthLowWeightSD="1.5" birthLowWeightPenalty="0.10"
                birthHighWeightSD="1.3" birthHighWeightPenalty="0.10"/>

    <!-- Chicks: about 40 g, ~65% of the egg. -->
    <animalType name="CHICKEN" birthWeightCV="0.08"
                birthSurvivalBase="0.96" birthHealthEffect="0.15"
                birthLowWeightSD="1.5" birthLowWeightPenalty="0.10"
                birthHighWeightSD="2.5" birthHighWeightPenalty="0">
        <breed name="CHICKEN" birthWeightRatio="0.0123" maleBirthFactor="1.05"/>  <!-- 40 / 42 g -->
    </animalType>

    <!--
        Growth line of animals raised for meat: bulls, rams, buck kids, cockerels, drakes, ganders,
        rabbits and gilts (the boars' is on the PIG breeds above). Each reaches its slaughter reference
        (target weight x maturity in carcassYield.xml) at a realistic age on full feed: TMR for cattle.
        Pigs, kids and birds are finished at the map butcher's youngest age (Hof Bergmann: pigs 6, goats 8,
        poultry 6, geese 10, rabbits 4 months); ram lambs at their real grass-system age (below). Cows, ewes, does and laying birds keep
        the mod's default (1.5 x the age they can first breed). Solved with tests/economics.py.
    -->
    <subType name="BULL_HOLSTEIN"        matureAgeMonth="40.2" priceTargetWeight="1000" priceMatureAgeMonth="32"/>  <!-- 649 kg at 17 months -->
    <subType name="BULL_LIMOUSIN"        matureAgeMonth="55.0" priceTargetWeight="1000" priceMatureAgeMonth="35.7"/>  <!-- 702 kg at 18 months -->
    <subType name="BULL_ANGUS"           matureAgeMonth="52.2" priceTargetWeight="850" priceMatureAgeMonth="33.4"/>  <!-- 601 kg at 19.5 months -->
    <subType name="BULL_HEREFORD"        matureAgeMonth="54.2" priceTargetWeight="850" priceMatureAgeMonth="35.9"/>  <!-- 571 kg at 19.5 months -->
    <subType name="BULL_SWISS_BROWN"     matureAgeMonth="51.5" priceTargetWeight="900" priceMatureAgeMonth="37.8"/>  <!-- 620 kg at 20 months -->
    <subType name="BULL_WATERBUFFALO"    matureAgeMonth="60.3" priceTargetWeight="900" priceMatureAgeMonth="45.4"/>  <!-- 450 kg at 23 months -->
    <subType name="BULL_HIGHLAND_CATTLE" matureAgeMonth="63.3" priceTargetWeight="750" priceMatureAgeMonth="40.2"/>  <!-- 480 kg at 25 months -->
    <subType name="PIG_LANDRACE"         matureAgeMonth="15.9"/>  <!-- gilt: 125 kg at 6 months -->
    <subType name="PIG_BLACK_PIED"       matureAgeMonth="10.0"/>  <!-- gilt: 120 kg at 6 months -->
    <subType name="PIG_BERKSHIRE"        matureAgeMonth="13.5"/>  <!-- gilt: 120 kg at 6.5 months -->
    <!-- Ram lambs (sheep S3, 2026-10-11): each reaches its real market weight (carcassYield.xml, 42.7 kg lowland, 38 kg
         hill) at the real grass-system age: about 13 weeks at dam on 250-300 g a day, then 200-250 g on grass (hill 232,
         then 150-200). Pack rams (FS25_SheepBreedsRLRM) had none and grew to their mature weight by 7.5 months. -->
    <subType name="RAM_LANDRACE"              matureAgeMonth="22.0"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.5 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_STEINSCHAF"            matureAgeMonth="13.7"/>  <!-- 38 kg at 5.5 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_SWISS_MOUNTAIN"        matureAgeMonth="16.8"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.8 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_BLACK_WELSH"           matureAgeMonth="10.4"/>  <!-- 35.4 kg at 5.1 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_TEXEL"                 matureAgeMonth="13.9"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.7 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_SUFFOLK"               matureAgeMonth="13.5"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.5 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_BLUE_FACED_LEICESTER"  matureAgeMonth="12.4"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.6 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_BORDER_LEICESTER"      matureAgeMonth="12.8"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.3 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_BELCLARE"              matureAgeMonth="11.4"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.7 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_DORSET"                matureAgeMonth="11.0"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.6 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_CHEVIOT"               matureAgeMonth="12.5"/>  <!-- 38 kg at 5.3 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_KERRY_HILL"            matureAgeMonth="12.6"/>  <!-- 38 kg at 5.4 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_NORFOLK"               matureAgeMonth="12.3"/>  <!-- 38 kg at 5.2 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_BLACKFACE"             matureAgeMonth="13.9"/>  <!-- 38 kg at 5.4 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_EASYCARE"              matureAgeMonth="10.7"/>  <!-- 42.7 kg at 4.8 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_JACOB"                 matureAgeMonth="10.6"/>  <!-- 38 kg at 5.3 months -->
    <subType name="RAM_GOAT"             matureAgeMonth="27.9"/>  <!-- buck kid: 35 kg at 8 months -->
    <subType name="CHICKEN_ROOSTER"      matureAgeMonth="8.7"/>   <!-- cockerel: 3.2 kg at 6 months -->
    <subType name="DUCK_MALE"            matureAgeMonth="7.3"/>   <!-- drake: 3.8 kg at 6 months (Hof Bergmann) -->
    <subType name="GOOSE_MALE"           matureAgeMonth="12.4"/>  <!-- gander: 7.2 kg at 10 months (Hof Bergmann) -->
    <subType name="RABBIT_MALE"          matureAgeMonth="6.6"/>   <!-- 3 kg at 4 months (Hof Bergmann) -->
    <subType name="RABBIT"               matureAgeMonth="7.3" femaleGrowth="1.0"/>  <!-- doe: 3 kg at 4 months -->

    <!--
        Live-price model. Animal:getSellPrice measures an animal's weight against a reference line
        (priceTargetWeight over priceMatureAgeMonth, the values the calibrated prices were made with) so
        that prices do not move when target weights or growth lines are set to real weights. The
        animal's weight is put on that line as its share of the full-feed growth line
        (RLBreedTraits.toPriceWeight): a dealer's animal or one on full feed is priced by age as before.
    -->
    <subType name="COW_SWISS_BROWN" priceTargetWeight="650" priceMatureAgeMonth="18"/>
    <subType name="COW_HOLSTEIN" priceTargetWeight="680" priceMatureAgeMonth="18"/>
    <subType name="COW_ANGUS" priceTargetWeight="650" priceMatureAgeMonth="18"/>
    <subType name="COW_LIMOUSIN" priceTargetWeight="650" priceMatureAgeMonth="18"/>
    <subType name="COW_HEREFORD" priceTargetWeight="600" priceMatureAgeMonth="18"/>
    <subType name="COW_HIGHLAND_CATTLE" priceTargetWeight="500" priceMatureAgeMonth="18"/>
    <subType name="COW_WATERBUFFALO" priceTargetWeight="550" priceMatureAgeMonth="18"/>

    <!-- Example of a single subtype:
    <subType name="BULL_HIGHLAND_CATTLE" growth="0.85"/>
    -->

    <!--
        milkScale: the Cow Breeds pack (FS25_CowBreedsRLRM) ships milk curves calibrated before the milk balance of 2026-10-11 (this
        mod's are 1.2 x the real lactation average, mature value = 1.2 x real kg/day x 30 / 1.08). The scale takes each pack curve
        to that level (the factor between the two curves): the pack's file stays as its author made it. The feed bridge divides by the
        scaled curve, so feed cost does not move. (Jersey: 667 game liters a month at maturity,
        Holstein-type Red Holstein about 820, the beef breeds 230-430.)
    -->
    <!--
        milkCollect: share of a cow's milk beyond what her own calves drink that reaches the milk tank or cans (0..1, default 1).
        Suckler (beef) breeds 0: a beef cow does give milk, but little, and in a cow-calf herd it is her calf's - there is no
        surplus worth collecting. In the real-equivalent nutrition mode such a cow makes only what her calves at foot drink
        (none without a calf) and is fed for that; under the old rules her store gets nothing and her budget is unchanged.
        Dairy and dual-purpose breeds, the water buffalo and goats keep 1.
    -->
    <subType name="COW_REDHOLSTEIN_PACK" milkScale="3.487"/>
    <subType name="COW_AYRSHIRE_PACK" milkScale="2.609"/>
    <subType name="COW_KERRY_PACK" milkScale="1.816"/>
    <subType name="COW_SHORTHORNMILKERS_PACK" milkScale="2.267"/>
    <subType name="COW_JERSEY_PACK" milkScale="2.578"/>
    <subType name="COW_GUERNSEY_PACK" milkScale="2.507"/>
    <subType name="COW_CHAROLAIS_PACK" milkScale="1.667" milkCollect="0"/>
    <subType name="COW_REDANGUS_PACK" milkScale="1.786" milkCollect="0"/>
    <subType name="COW_SHORTHORN_PACK" milkScale="1.506" milkCollect="0"/>
    <subType name="COW_IRISHMOILED_PACK" milkScale="1.296" milkCollect="0"/>
    <subType name="COW_BRITISHBLUE_PACK" milkScale="1.553" milkCollect="0"/>
    <subType name="COW_BELTEDGALLOWAY_PACK" milkScale="1.340" milkCollect="0"/>
    <subType name="COW_SIMMENTAL_PACK" milkScale="1.693" milkCollect="0"/>
    <!--
        Sheep curve scales (sheep S3, 2026-10-11; RLCurveScale, applied once after every bridge and pack has loaded).
        woolScale: wool = 1.2 x the breed's real fleece a year (0.76 kg/L), spread over 12 months (no cold gate).
        waterScale / strawScale / manureScale: the cattle ratio - sheep game value = COW_ANGUS game value x (sheep real /
        cattle real) at the same state: water 5.25 L/day for an 80 kg ewe (x weight^0.75) vs 38 L for a dry beef cow,
        straw 1.0 kg/day (hill breeds 0.65) vs 8.33 kg, excreta 0.040 vs 0.0591 kg per kg weight a day.
        priceScale (rams, both price curves): a dealer ram lamb at its real finishing age earns +12% a year by contract
        slaughter over selling it live at SHEEPGOATMEAT 14.95 (the calibration PR #22 made at 8 months); solved with the real
        price code by FS25_SheepBreeds_离线准备_2026-10-11/tools/calibrate_ram_prices.py.
        Values and sources: FS25_SheepBreeds_离线准备_2026-10-11/change-set-v1.json. Goats are not scaled.
    -->
    <subType name="SHEEP_LANDRACE"            woolScale="0.00712" waterScale="0.4452" strawScale="0.3901" manureScale="1.5342"/>  <!-- wool 0.605 L/month (5.52 kg a year), water 26.7, straw 15.6, manure 53.7 -->
    <subType name="RAM_LANDRACE"              woolScale="0.01053" waterScale="0.5521" strawScale="0.3901" manureScale="2.5105" priceScale="1.5035"/>  <!-- wool 0.895 L/month (8.16 kg a year), water 38.6, straw 15.6, manure 87.9 -->
    <subType name="SHEEP_STEINSCHAF"          woolScale="0.00564" waterScale="0.2682" strawScale="0.2536" manureScale="0.9589"/>  <!-- wool 0.395 L/month (3.6 kg a year), water 18.8, straw 10.1, manure 33.6 -->
    <subType name="RAM_STEINSCHAF"            woolScale="0.00658" waterScale="0.3118" strawScale="0.2536" manureScale="1.3076" priceScale="1.4999"/>  <!-- wool 0.461 L/month (4.2 kg a year), water 23.7, straw 10.1, manure 45.8 -->
    <subType name="SHEEP_SWISS_MOUNTAIN"      woolScale="0.00909" waterScale="0.2703" strawScale="0.3901" manureScale="1.2553"/>  <!-- wool 0.5 L/month (4.56 kg a year), water 23, straw 15.6, manure 43.9 -->
    <subType name="RAM_SWISS_MOUNTAIN"        woolScale="0.01136" waterScale="0.3500" strawScale="0.3901" manureScale="1.8829" priceScale="1.5536"/>  <!-- wool 0.625 L/month (5.7 kg a year), water 31.1, straw 15.6, manure 65.9 -->
    <subType name="SHEEP_BLACK_WELSH"         woolScale="0.00263" waterScale="0.3237" strawScale="0.2536" manureScale="0.8717"/>  <!-- wool 0.25 L/month (2.28 kg a year), water 17.5, straw 10.1, manure 30.5 -->
    <subType name="RAM_BLACK_WELSH"           woolScale="0.00329" waterScale="0.3796" strawScale="0.2536" manureScale="1.1855" priceScale="1.4614"/>  <!-- wool 0.312 L/month (2.85 kg a year), water 22, straw 10.1, manure 41.5 -->
    <subType name="SHEEP_TEXEL"               woolScale="0.00899" waterScale="0.2925" strawScale="0.3901" manureScale="1.3076"/>  <!-- wool 0.539 L/month (4.92 kg a year), water 23.7, straw 15.6, manure 45.8 -->
